  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Sunday)\n\nInteresting upper level pattern beginning to develop for the \nwestern US as the subtropical high relaxes its grip across the \nsilver state. The summertime subtropical ridge that has been \nanchored over the eastern great basin is beginning to shift which \nis allowing some monsoonal moisture to work its way into central \nNevada. This will help lower temperatures to more seasonal values \nand with the moisture rain chances due to afternoon and evening \nthunderstorms will be prevalent this week. Monday and Tuesday \nafternoon will have a 20% to 30% chance for thunderstorms mainly \nalong the US 50 corridor. These storms will be slow moving, \nallowing for a higher chances for rain to reach the ground given \nthe dry surface layer as wet storms may produce up to 0.10\u201d of \nrain. Still there will be a few dry thunderstorms that form, with \nan elevated risk for lightning fire starts. Wednesday a digging \nupper level trough will work its way into California, Models show \nthat this system will help Nevada out in multiple ways, first \ndropping temperatures significantly through the end of the week \nwith highs returning to the low 80s to low 90s. Second, it will \nhelp channel monsoonal moisture flow increasing chances for wetter\nstorm modes. Finally, the added dynamic cooling and dynamic lift \nwill also help to increase overall precipitation chances for mid \nto late week. The only question that is unresolved is how far \nnorth and west the best moisture gets advected over NV. Current \nthinking has all but far NW Humboldt County seeing a chance at \nwetting rains (\u22650.10\u201d) this week. However, ensemble model members \nare split on this scenario as some show all of Humboldt getting in\non rain, whereas some show most of N NV missing out. After \nSaturday the upper trough lifts NE into Canada which may lower \nstorm chances a bit for the weekend, but models are split on the \nupper pattern after Sunday as the GFS favors keeping the western \nUS under some form of troughing aloft, while the ECMWF shows that \nthe upper ridge will again shift back west after a few days in the\nsouthern plains. \n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: \n \nThere is high confidence for chances of isolated to scattered mix\nof wet and dry thunderstorms Through Friday afternoon. \n\nThere moderate confidence for isolated to scattered mix of wet \nand dry thunderstorms for NE Nevada Wednesday through Friday.\n\nThere is low confidence for isolated mix of wet or dry \nthunderstorms for NW Nevada Wednesday through Friday. \n\nThere is low confidence in the evolution of the upper pattern \nafter Sunday as models are disagreement as to the strength and \nlocation of key upper level features.",
